timestamp=2026-11-08T22:34:18.875Z level=INFO run=50327b16 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Let us write a simple parser for this ASCII tree. One common approach is to track\n# the positions of names and the connecting lines. The tree uses |, +, -, \\, / etc.\n# and names appear as text. We need to build the parent-child graph.\n\npath = '/app/dataset/genealogy_trees.txt'\nwith open(path) as f:\n    lines = f.readlines()\n\n# Find all person names and their positions (row, col)\nimport re\n\npersons = []\nname_pattern = re.compile(r'([A-ZĄĆĘŁŃÓŚŹŻ][a-ząćęłńóśźżA-ZĄĆĘŁŃÓŚŹŻ\\'-]*\\s+[A-ZĄĆĘŁŃÓŚŹŻ][a-ząćęłńóśźżA-ZĄĆĘŁŃÓŚŹŻ\\'-]*)')\nfor i, line in enumerate(lines):\n    for m in name_pattern.finditer(line):\n        name = m.group(1)\n        # filter out \"FAMILY TREE\" etc if any\n        if name in ('FAMILY TREE', 'FAMILY GENERATOR'):\n            continue\n        persons.append((i, m.start(), m.end(), name))\n\n# Deduplicate by name? maybe same name appears multiple times? unlikely, but possible\n# Build set of unique names\nunique = {}\nfor row, start, end, name in persons:\n    if name not in unique:\n        unique[name] = (row, start, end)\n    else:\n        # keep first occurrence? or maybe the one that is more central\n        pass\n\nprint(len(unique))\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
